    "body": "Great on any Friday: Motorola Sound MA1 - Android Auto Wireless Adapter\nRivian R2 and R1 Model Family Recalled for Incorrect Tire Placard Labels\n6 photos\nPhoto: Rivian\nRivian has determined that certain tire placards and certification labels may not describe the wheel-and-tire configurations R1 and R2 customers actually received. A grand total of 1,619 vehicles are being recalled because of this, with Rivian calling this recall FSAM-1900.\nReferred to as 26V761 by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, the campaign is more serious than it seems. If the recommended inflation pressures are incorrect, over or underinflation increases the risk of a crash. Too much pressure causes the middle of the tire to bulge outward, shrinking the contact patch. Overinflation also means the tire can't absorb bumps properly.\nMore worryingly, an overinflated tire has no give, making it prone to a sudden blowout. Underinflation is pretty bad as well in this context, for an underinflated tire builds too much internal heat while flexing. Too little pressure also leads to poor handling, for soft sidewalls bend easily when turning the steering wheel. Of course, longer stopping distances also have to be highlighted.\nAnd lastly, improper tire pressures may squeeze the grooves shut instead of channeling water away. This may lead to hydroplaning, and nobody wants that in a vehicle as heavy as a Rivian. Per the recall documentation, the manufacturing dates of the subject vehicles are spread between October 18, 202,1 for the R1T pickup and August 25, 2026, for the R2 sport utility vehicle.\nHow did Rivian deliver these vehicles with incorrect labels? The automaker's official explanation points to modifications that were made between production and customer delivery. More specifically, a bunch of vehicles may have received a different wheel, tire, and/or spare tire configuration without receiving the corresponding placard information changes.\nPhoto: Rivian It goes without saying that equipment changes are possible between initial assembly and customer delivery. There's nothing inherently wrong with offering a bunch of configurations, but it sure is wrong when the documentation doesn't keep up with such changes until the first owner takes delivery of the vehicle.\nRL00002368-A is the part number for the tire-and-loading label, while RL00000004-D designates the certification label. The recall condition falls under Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard 110 for all things wheel and tire selection. Its requirements establish the details that automakers must provide so customers can identify the appropriate tire dimensions, pressures, and wheel setup.\nNaturally, the remedy involves replacing the affected tire placard and/or certification label with updated versions containing he correct information. The electric vehicle manufacturer intends to notify customers by January 18, 2027, the day affected VINs are scheduled to become searchable on the NHTSA's portal.\nRecently crowned MotorTrend's 2027 sport utility vehicle of the year, the R2 carries a starting price of $44,990 for the Standard, $53,990 for the Premium, and $57,990 for the Performance specification. The latter needs 3.6 seconds to hit 60 miles per hour (97 kilometers per hour), while maximum range favors it and the Premium. Both top 330 miles (531 kilometers) on a full charge.\nNot bad for such a boxy vehicle. The R1S can do better still. It goes 410 miles (660 kilometers) at most between charges, while the R1T is listed with 420 miles (676 kilometers).\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nrivian r1s rivian R1T rivian r2 rivian recall\nDownload: Rivian tire placard label recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Six-Grader Saw Rare 1966 Pontiac GTO 4-Speed Tri-Power, Restored It to MCACN Gold Decades Later",
